          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
        rules: {
          objective: "Complete a winning hand of five pairs (one identical 'Eye' and four 10/20-sum pairs).",
          gameplay: "1. Drawing and discarding proceed counter-clockwise.\n2. In K'ap Tái Shap, any player can shout 'K'ap!' to take a discard out of turn IF it completes their winning hand (similar to Mahjong).",
          keyRules: [
            'A winning hand requires exactly one identical pair (ngán/eye) from either Civil or Military series',
            'Four additional pairs must each total exactly 10 or 20 pips',
            'The 4-2 tile counts as 3 pips (not 6) when calculating pair totals',
            "Players may call 'K'ap!' to claim a discard out of turn if it completes their hand",
            'First player starts with 10 tiles; others start with 9 tiles',
            'Discards are placed face-up and available for any player to claim',
          ],
          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
        strategy: {
          basic: "With multiple sets, it is much easier to find an identical 'Eye'. Focus on the 'Sum-Pairs' first, as they determine the flow of your hand.",
          intermediate: 'Hoarding: If you see that 6-6 tiles are appearing frequently in discards, start hoarding 4-4 or 3-1 to complete 10/20 sums. The Big game is about recognizing high-volume numbers.',
          advanced: "Defensive Discarding: Observe the 'Sum' your neighbors are building. If your neighbor is picking up low-value tiles (1s and 2s), don't discard any 8s or 9s—you are likely giving them their 10-sum.",
          tips: [
            'The 4-2 tile is extremely powerful in the Big variant because it can bridge both 10 and 20 target sums depending on whether you count it as 6 or 3.',
            "Don't wait for a 'Three Nobles' combination unless you have 3/4 of the pieces; it's too slow for this fast variant.",
          ],
          hasPlaceholders: false,
